Reflection on Hyperparameter Tuning Strategy (Round 7)

In this iteration, I focused my hyperparameter tuning efforts on those most directly affecting the surrogate model’s ability to balance exploration and exploitation. In particular, I prioritised kernel choice and kernel length-scale in the Gaussian Process model, along with the assumed noise level and acquisition function parameters. These hyperparameters strongly influence smoothness assumptions, uncertainty estimation and how aggressively the model explores uncertain regions, making them especially important as the data set grows.

Compared to earlier rounds, hyperparameter tuning has made my query strategy more deliberate and less heuristic. In the initial rounds, my approach leaned heavily toward broad exploration because limited data made model assumptions unreliable. With 16 data points now available, tuning allowed me to trust the surrogate model more and place queries in regions that balance local exploitation near promising optima with targeted exploration where uncertainty remains high. This represents a shift from intuition-driven sampling toward model-informed decision-making.

The primary tuning methods I applied were manual adjustment and limited Bayesian optimisation. Manual tuning was useful for quickly testing the sensitivity of outcomes to changes in length-scale and noise assumptions, especially when computational budgets were constrained. Bayesian optimisation of hyperparameters provided a more principled way to refine these settings but came with higher computational cost and diminishing returns as the number of evaluations increased. This trade-off highlighted an important practical consideration: while automated tuning methods can improve performance, they must be balanced against time and resource constraints.

As the data set expanded to 16 points, several model limitations became clearer through tuning. Overfitting emerged as a risk when overly small length-scales were selected, leading the model to fit noise rather than true structure. Additionally, some query dimensions appeared less influential than initially assumed, suggesting that certain features may be effectively irrelevant or weakly informative. Finally, diminishing performance gains from aggressive tuning indicated that model improvements are increasingly constrained by the amount and distribution of data rather than hyperparameter choice alone.

Looking ahead, for larger data sets or more complex models, I would apply scalable hyperparameter tuning techniques such as random search or early-stopping methods like Hyperband to manage computational cost. For higher-dimensional problems, hierarchical tuning strategies or priors informed by domain knowledge would help reduce the effective search space. These approaches would allow hyperparameter tuning to remain feasible while still improving model robustness.

Working in this black-box optimisation setting closely mirrors real-world ML practice, where true objective functions are unknown and data is costly to obtain. Hyperparameter tuning in this context reinforces the importance of uncertainty estimation, cautious interpretation of model outputs and iterative refinement under incomplete information. It encourages thinking probabilistically, recognising model limitations and making decisions that balance risk and reward all essential skills for professional ML and AI practitioners operating in real-world, high-stakes environments.
